<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huldrych Zwingli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1484">1484</a>–<a href="/years/1531">1531</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ulrich Zwingli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huldrych Zwingli was a Swiss priest and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in Zürich. After serving as parish priest at Glarus, he moved to Zürich in 1519, where his preaching led the city to abolish the Mass in 1525. His teachings shaped Reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. He died as a chaplain with the Zürich army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kappel">Battle of Kappel</a> in 1531.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli.jpg" alt="Huldrych Zwingli" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Asper · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulrich-Zwingli-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zwingli was born on 1 January 1484 at Wildhaus in the Toggenburg valley, the third of eleven children in a farming family. His father Ulrich was the chief local magistrate. At ten he was sent to Basel for secondary schooling. He studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> and then at Basel, where he received his Master of Arts in 1506. He served as a pastor in Glarus and later in Einsiedeln, and the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> influenced him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 1 January 1519 Zwingli gave his first sermon as people's priest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grossmunster">Grossmünster</a> in Zurich. Instead of preaching on the Sunday's set Gospel, he read through Matthew verse by verse, and went on through the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> and the Old. In Lent 1522 he and about a dozen others ate sausages in breach of the fast, and the Affair of the Sausages is considered the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. After the first Zurich disputation, on 29 January 1523, the city council allowed him to go on preaching. Zurich severed its ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Constance in 1524 and replaced the Mass with a new communion liturgy in 1525.</p><p>Zwingli clashed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a>, and after a public debate on 17 January 1525 the council ordered those who refused to have their children baptised to leave Zurich. A Christian Civic Union of Reformed cities was formed in 1528. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg-colloquy">Marburg Colloquy</a> in 1529 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> agreed on many points but not on the Real Presence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, which Zwingli took to be symbolic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1531 the Reformed alliance's food <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of the Catholic cantons failed. The cantons attacked when Zurich was ill-prepared, and Zwingli died on the battlefield on 11 October 1531. The Zurich council chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger">Heinrich Bullinger</a> as his successor, who defended him as a prophet and martyr and consolidated his reforms. Zwingli's legacy lives on in the confessions, liturgy and church orders of the Reformed churches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huldrych Zwingli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Huldrych Zwingli</b>, people’s priest at the Grossmünster from 1519, led the first Reformation in the Swiss Confederacy: after the public disputation of January 1523 the council adopted his programme, images were removed from the churches and in 1525 the Mass was replaced by a simple communion service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Huldrych Zwingli</b> began reform in Zürich from 1519, and the allied city of Geneva, where John Calvin worked from 1536 and founded his academy in 1559, became the centre of Reformed Protestantism, whose ideas spread to France, the Netherlands, Scotland, Hungary and the English-speaking world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Zurich <b>Huldrych Zwingli</b> taught that the Lord’s Supper was a memorial of Christ’s sacrifice, while Luther insisted that Christ’s body and blood were truly present in the bread and wine; at the Marburg Colloquy of 1529 they agreed on fourteen articles of faith but not on this…</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Martin Luther translated the New Testament into German from Erasmus’s Greek text, and <b>Huldrych Zwingli</b>, Melanchthon and John Calvin were humanists by training, as were many Catholic reformers.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Kappel am Albis, the Catholic cantons of central Switzerland defeated Protestant Zürich, and the reformer <b>Huldrych Zwingli</b>, who accompanied the Zürich army as chaplain, was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-11">11 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On 11 October they defeated Zürich at Kappel, where the reformer <b>Huldrych Zwingli</b> was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wildhaus, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Died in Kappel am Albis, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.2,9.35&amp;year=1514" aria-label="Wildhaus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4769 507 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Huldrych Zwingli"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5219 777h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5219 777h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.2,9.35&amp;year=1514">What was here in 1514? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Those who, for truth, religion, justice, and native country, venture their lives in war, are true men, and their cause is sacred.&#8221;</p><footer>Exhortation to the Swiss Confederates against foreign mercenary service &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/On_Mercenary_Soldiers" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…as creation itself came from the hand of the all-working God at the beginning, so that, in external things, there is nothing in the universe so like God as the worker.&#8221;</p><footer>Exhortation to the Swiss Confederates against foreign mercenary service &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; of work &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/On_Mercenary_Soldiers" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For where God is not in the heart there is nothing but the man himself. Where there is nothing but the man himself, he cares for nothing but that which serves to his interests, pleasures, and lusts.&#8221;</p><footer>Exhortation to the Swiss Confederates against foreign mercenary service &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/On_Mercenary_Soldiers" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
